%% Abstract
\begin{abstract}
Signal reliability analysis in process systems requires computing the probability that viable paths exist from source nodes to the target. Traditional methods struggle with reconvergent path structures where multiple paths from common sources reconverge at downstream nodes, creating statistical dependencies that violate independence assumptions underlying standard reliability calculations.

This paper presents the Information Propagation Algorithm (IPA), an exact message-passing approach that resolves these dependencies through conditional enumeration on shared fork ancestors. IPA processes networks in topological layers, applying inclusion-exclusion for independent paths and progressive diamond decomposition for dependent structures. Diamond subgraphs are systematically identified, solved through conditional probability enumeration, and replaced with statistically equivalent supernodes, reducing computational complexity from $O(2^{\sum c_i} \times \prod d_i)$ to $O(\sum 2^{c_i})$.

Two case studies are presented to demonstrate the computational advantages. On a 16-node grid network, IPA achieves 134-times faster execution than dPrPm while maintaining exact results. Analysis of six Pareto-optimal drone logistics network configurations demonstrates that computation time depends on diamond nesting depth rather than network size: shallow-nested networks (depth $\leq$12) complete in under one second while deeply nested structures exhibit the exponential growth inherent to exact inference in high-treewidth graphs. The results show that exact signal reception reliability is computed for all nodes in reasonable time, indicating that IPA is a useful network analysis tool for performance analysis and maintenance planning in multi-source process systems.
\end{abstract}


%%Research highlights
\begin{highlights}
\item Introduces an exact reliability computation for directed acyclic process networks with complex dependency structures
\item Proposes a progressive diamond decomposition through conditional enumeration on shared fork ancestors 
\item Reduces computational complexity from $O(2^{\sum c_i} \times \prod d_i)$ to $O(\sum 2^{c_i})$ through super node replacement
\item Demonstrates 134-times faster execution than existing message-passing methods while maintaining exact results
\item Applied to realistic drone logistics networks with varying diamond complexity, demonstrating tractability boundaries for exact inference
\end{highlights}


