Without decomposition, resolving these types of subgraphs would involve simultaneously conditioning on the combination of states of every shared fork node. In diamonds with long paths and multiple intermediate convergence points, this quickly becomes intractable. For nested diamond structures, this requires multi-level conditional enumeration:

\begin{align}
b(n_j) = R(n_j) \times \sum_{s_1=0}^{2^{m_1}-1} \sum_{s_2=0}^{2^{m_2}-1} \cdots \sum_{s_L=0}^{2^{m_L}-1} &P(\text{state}_{s_1}) \times P(\text{state}_{s_2}|\text{state}_{s_1}) \nonumber \\
&\times \cdots \times \phi_L(\text{state}_{s_1} \ldots \text{state}_{s_L})
\end{align}

where $L$ represents the number of nesting levels, $m_i$ is the number of conditioning nodes at level $i$, and the computation scales exponentially with both the depth and breadth of nested structures.

IPA’s progressive decomposition avoids this exponential complexity by exploiting the path exhaustive property of diamonds: once an inner diamond is solved, its conditional probabilities remain valid regardless of how outer diamonds condition on different states. This allows diamond structures to be replaced with supernodes that preserve exact mathematical relationships while eliminating the need for repeated conditioning. The following lemmas establish the mathematical foundation for the decomposition method.


\begin{lemma}{Invariance of Inner Diamond}
Let $D_{\text{outer}}$ be a diamond containing an inner diamond $D_{\text{inner}}$ where the join node of $D_{\text{inner}}$ is an intermediate node in the paths of $D_{\text{outer}}$. Then the conditional probabilities $P(J_{\text{inner}}|F_{\text{inner}})$ and $P(J_{\text{inner}}|\bar{F}_{\text{inner}})$ are invariant to the conditioning state of $D_{\text{outer}}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By the diamond definition, $D_{\text{inner}}$ contains all paths between its fork and join nodes. The conditional probabilities depend only on the internal structure of $D_{\text{inner}}$, which remains unchanged regardless of the outer diamond's conditioning state. When $D_{\text{outer}}$ conditions on fork state $s$, the inner diamond's probability becomes:
\begin{align}
P(\text{signal through } D_{\text{inner}}) &= P(J_{\text{inner}}|F_{\text{inner}}) \cdot b(F_{\text{inner}}|s) \nonumber \\
&\quad + P(J_{\text{inner}}|\bar{F}_{\text{inner}}) \cdot (1-b(F_{\text{inner}}|s))
\end{align}
where $b(F_{\text{inner}}|s)$ is the fork belief given outer conditioning state $s$, but the conditional terms $P(J_{\text{inner}}|F_{\text{inner}})$ and $P(J_{\text{inner}}|\bar{F}_{\text{inner}})$ remain constant.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Supernode Equivalence]
Let $D_{\text{inner}}$ be a diamond with fork node $F_{\text{inner}}$ and join node $J_{\text{inner}}$. When $D_{\text{inner}}$ is replaced with a supernode, the supernode stores the conditional probabilities $P(J_{\text{inner}}|F_{\text{inner}})$ and $P(J_{\text{inner}}|\bar{F}_{\text{inner}})$. For any outer conditioning context with fork belief $b(F_{\text{inner}}|s)$, the supernode's contribution is:
\begin{align}
P(\text{supernode contribution}) &= P(J_{\text{inner}}|F_{\text{inner}}) \cdot b(F_{\text{inner}}|s) \nonumber \\
&\quad + P(J_{\text{inner}}|\bar{F}_{\text{inner}}) \cdot (1-b(F_{\text{inner}}|s))
\end{align}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The conditional probabilities $P(J_{\text{inner}}|F_{\text{inner}})$ and $P(J_{\text{inner}}|\bar{F}_{\text{inner}})$ depend only on $D_{\text{inner}}$'s internal structure and remain invariant across different conditioning contexts. By storing these conditional terms rather than a fixed probability, the supernode can correctly compute its contribution for any fork belief value $b(F_{\text{inner}}|s)$ determined by the outer diamond's conditioning state $s$. This preserves the exact mathematical relationship that would exist if $D_{\text{inner}}$ were processed directly within each conditioning context.
\end{proof}


The diamond-to-supernode decomposition serves two purposes: first, it eliminates the need to re-condition on the same diamond if it appears as a part of a larger diamond’s intermediate nodes. Secondly, collapsing the diamond edges always simplifies the network structure for later diamonds further in the topological sorting (Table~\ref{tab:complexity_comparison}). 


To further illustrate the diamond to supernode decomposition process, consider the nested diamond network shown in Figure~\ref{fig:progressive_decomposition}:

The topological processing order guarantees that node 6’s belief is updated before any deeper join nodes. When processing node 6, the algorithm discovers diamond D1, which consists of the shared fork ancestor node 1 with divergent paths 1→2→5→6 and 1→3→6. The diamond is solved by conditioning only on node 1's state. Once solved, the entire diamond subgraph (nodes 2, 3, 5, 6, 13) is replaced with supernode D1. With D1 reduced to a supernode,the algorithm continues with a reduced network structure. 
The same process is repeated later for D2 and D3 with each decomposition step reducing the computational burden for later diamonds. By the time the algorithm reaches the final sink node 16, only $O(2^1)$ conditioning states (node 1 active/inactive) are needed since supernodes D2 and D3 have absorbed and preserved the dependency introduced from the inner fork nodes 7 and 14. 


