\section{Case Studies}
\subsection{Comparison to dPrPm Approach}

Both our Information Propagation Algorithm (IPA) and Tong and Tien's directed Probability Propagation Method (dPrPm)~\cite{tong_probability_2019} aim to evaluate network reliability through message propagation. The similarities between these approaches stem from the fundamental acyclic nature of directed acyclic graphs. Both methods:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Process nodes in a topologically sorted order to ensure dependencies are properly handled,
    \item Leverage the network's directional structure to propagate probabilities from source to sink nodes, and
    \item Aim to reduce computational complexity compared to traditional enumeration methods.
\end{itemize}

However, the methods diverge in their implementation of how messages between nodes are computed. In dPrPm, when multiple predecessors influence a node, the update rules explicitly rely on approximating a three-node joint distribution for every new edge introduced. These rules are fundamental when the incoming messages are combined in an intermediate structure, where the new link addition affects nodes that have already received some information, requiring a more nuanced treatment of shared dependencies. Although effective, this approach requires storing both marginal and pairwise probabilities throughout the network, which can become memory-intensive in large or dense networks.

IPA takes a fundamentally different view of network dependencies by explicitly modelling them through diamond structures. Rather than tracking pairwise relationships, it identifies and decomposes these structures into statistically equivalent supernodes.  As a result, IPA avoids maintaining or updating pairwise dependencies at complex intersection points, since the relevant structure is captured inherently in the decomposition.

